<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olaf II of Norway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 995–<a href="/years/1030">1030</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Saint Olaf, Olaf Haraldsson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Olaf Haraldsson, later Saint Olaf, was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1015 to 1028. A former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raider, he drove out the earls of Lade, subdued the petty kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppland">Upplands</a> around 1018 and pressed the conversion of Norway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> drove him out in 1028, and he was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stiklestad">Battle of Stiklestad</a> in 1030 trying to regain the throne. Venerated as a saint, he became Norway's patron.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway.jpg" alt="Olaf II of Norway" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olav_der_Heilige07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Olaf Haraldsson was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asta-gudbrandsdatter">Åsta Gudbrandsdatter</a> and Harald Grenske, a petty king in Vestfold, who died while Åsta was pregnant. According to later Icelandic sagas, Harald descended from King Harald I Fairhair, so Olaf was a great-great-grandson of the founder of the Norwegian kingdom. Åsta later married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-syr">Sigurd Syr</a>, and their children included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a>. During his lifetime Olaf was known as Olaf the stout.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>'s Heimskringla, whose facts are dubious, he landed on the Estonian island of Saaremaa in 1008 and won a battle there. Skaldic poetry suggests he led an attack that took down London Bridge, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> sources do not confirm it, and he is said to have aided the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a> against Cnut.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Olaf was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1015 to 1028. Traditionally he has been seen as leading the Christianisation of Norway, but most scholars now believe he had little to do with it. Stone crosses suggest the coasts were deeply influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> long before him. He did import <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Normandy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and probably tried to enforce Christianity in the inland areas. The church laws later ascribed to him probably were not his.</p><p>Modern historians generally agree that Olaf was inclined to violence and brutality, and that he used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to gain more power for the monarchy and centralise control. Judged on the bare facts, he appears to have been a fairly unsuccessful ruler, whose power rested on an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a>, who was driven into exile when he claimed power of his own, and whose attempt at reconquest was swiftly crushed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Olaf died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stiklestad">Battle of Stiklestad</a> on 29 July 1030. Bishop Grimketel, who had come with him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, performed his canonisation about a year later, and he was enshrined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidaros-cathedral">Nidaros Cathedral</a>. He became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s patron saint and is also patron saint of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a>. He came to be called Rex Perpetuus Norvegiae, Norway's Eternal King, a title that goes back to the thirteenth century.</p><p>Historians point to three factors in his later status: the myth of his role in the conversion, dynastic relations and a later need for legitimacy. His illegitimate son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-the-good">Magnus the Good</a> took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and promoted his father's cult, which became typical of Scandinavian monarchies. The axe in Norway's coat of arms symbolises him, and Olsok, on 29 July, is his day of celebration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Olaf Haraldsson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Haakon the Good, raised at the court of Æthelstan of England, ruled in the mid-tenth century; Olaf Tryggvason (r. 995–1000) forced conversion on the coastal districts until his death at the Battle of Svolder; and <b>Olaf Haraldsson</b> (r. 1015–1028) completed the work of Christianisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Norway, the kings Olaf Tryggvason, at the end of the 10th century, and <b>Olaf Haraldsson</b>, killed at the battle of Stiklestad in 1030 and soon venerated as a saint, imposed Christianity, often by force.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In Norway Olaf Tryggvason, king from 995 to 1000, and <b>Olaf Haraldsson</b>, killed at Stiklestad in 1030 and soon venerated as Saint Olaf, imposed the new faith by force; Iceland’s Althing accepted it by agreement in 999 or 1000; and Sweden converted more gradually, into the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Such a retainer was a housecarl (húskarl) or a drengr, a “warrior”; the skald Sigvatr Þórðarson called the retainers of <b>Olaf II of Norway</b> heiðþegar, “receivers of pay”.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li><li><blockquote>At Stiklestad in Trøndelag, King <b>Olaf II of Norway</b>, returning from exile to reclaim his throne, was killed by an army of farmers and chieftains loyal to Cnut the Great.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-29">29 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Olaf Haraldsson</b> drove out the earls in 1015.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-lade">Earldom of Lade</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ringerike, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. Died in Stiklestad, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=60.21,10.15&amp;year=1017" aria-label="Ringerike on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Olaf II of Norway"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5429 4949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5429 4949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=60.21,10.15&amp;year=1017">What was here in 1017? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/asta-gudbrandsdatter"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/asta-gudbrandsdatter.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Åsta Gudbrandsdatter</span><span class="tp">Mother</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/magnus-the-good"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Magnus the Good</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; c. 1024–1047</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We will have all our men distinguished by a mark, so as to be a field-token upon their helmets and shields, by painting the holy cross thereupon with white colour.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to his army before Stiklestad, 1030 (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri's saga, written about 1230 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When we come into battle we shall all have one countersign and field-cry,—'Forward, forward, Christian men! cross men! king's men!&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to his army before Stiklestad, 1030 (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri's saga, written about 1230 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;That other foot was so much uglier than this one by having five ugly toes upon it, and this has only four; and now I have won the choice of asking something from thee.&#8221;</p><footer>Saga of Olaf Haraldson (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; his ruling on a bet with the Icelander Thorarin over whose foot was uglier, as Snorri Sturluson tells it two centuries later &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…remain here, and see the circumstances which may take place, and then ye will not have to follow the reports of others in what ye afterwards tell or sing concerning it.&#8221;</p><footer>Words to his skalds before the battle of Stiklestad, 1030 (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Olaf Haraldson) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri Sturluson's account, written two centuries later &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Thou art a very lucky man, Sigvat; but it is not wonderful that luck should accompany understanding. It is only wonderful how it sometimes happens that luck attends ignorant men, and that foolish counsel turns out lucky.&#8221;</p><footer>Words to the skald Sigvat (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Olaf Haraldson) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri Sturluson's account; Sigvat had named the king's newborn son Magnus without leave &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The time may come, friend, when thou wilt command ships.&#8221;</p><footer>Words to his three-year-old half-brother (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Olaf Haraldson) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri Sturluson's account; the boy was the future Harald Hardrada, playing with chips of wood as warships &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
